# DINE — Product North-Star (read before ANY feature work)

**DINE is a purpose-built interactive-fiction engine on Claude (via OpenRouter)** — not a chatbot, not a SillyTavern config. One human **authors** a story, then **plays** it: an AI **Narrator** writes prose, autonomous **NPC** agents act in-character from their own cards, and a hidden **beat document** steers toward chapter goals *without* issuing explicit plot instructions. Safety rests on **context isolation + three leak guards + one shared review gate**. Two data realms: **authoring** (immutable at runtime) and **save** (mutable per playthrough).

Canonical sources (read these, don't reconstruct intent from code): `docs/README.md`, `docs/directed_interactive_novel_engine_v2.html`, `docs/guides/glossary.md`, the relevant `docs/adr/*`, and `docs/adr/GAPS.md`.

## The two workflows

**A. Authoring — offline, BEFORE play** (ADR 0013 / 0018 / 0019)
Source bible → LLM-assisted compile → **review gate** → committed authoring rows (character cards, registers, sensitivities, lorebook, outline → chapters/scenes/beats). Authoring compiles enqueue with **no session**.

**B. Play — the runtime turn loop** (ADR 0016)

```
NARRATOR writes prose  →  handoff { PLAYER_MOMENT | NPC_MOMENT | BEAT_COMPLETE }
   → RECORDER commits surface + true_state + witnesses
   → APPRAISAL (per present character) emits delta / emotion proposals → REVIEW GATE
   → RUPTURES apply immediately in-scene · DRIFT is batched at SCENE_DONE
PLAYER_MOMENT: the human writes input + delivery, then the loop continues.
```

## Where the review gate lives — DO NOT get this wrong

The review gate is an **inline checkpoint**: AI-generated *proposals* are accepted / edited / rejected **before commit**. It fires **inside the play loop** (right after the AI responds, before anything is committed) and **inside authoring compile screens** — it is the human "fidelity floor" on everything the engine generates.

- ✅ Surfaced **where the work happens**: in-play at scene boundaries / ruptures; in the compile screen during authoring.
- ❌ **NOT** a standalone destination the player must visit every turn to approve, then navigate back. That round-trip is not the product.

The full inline review UX (batching, per-producer diff) is **Phase 7 / Epic E4**; the **O4 UI ADR is still open**. Any standalone `/reviews` page is **foundation / triage only**, never the play surface.

## Hard rules for every session

1. **Read first.** Before changing any feature, read `docs/README.md` + the relevant ADR(s) + `docs/adr/GAPS.md`. The agent's memory does not survive sessions — the docs do.
2. **Do not invent product surfaces.** If a UI placement is not pinned in an ADR (O4 is open), check it against the play/authoring workflow above AND confirm with the author before building it.
3. **Keep the whole-product narrative in view.** A sprint slice must fit the play loop and authoring flow — not merely pass its own tests in isolation.
